/E (C.  Barriers to Entry)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (       In order to safeguard the company and gain a competitive advantage in the market, the Solorcool Umbrella benefits from a number of important barriers to entry. First, the integration of efficient solar panels, battery systems, and cooling mechanisms, among other high technology costs, creates a financial barrier for new entrants trying to replicate the product's quality and functionality. Second, intellectual property for exclusive fan designs, energy management components, and solar-powered cooling systems are examples of intellectual property that can legally protect innovation and stop direct copying. Lastly, brand loyalty is important. By delivering a trustworthy, high-performing product and developing a strong brand identity focused on comfort, sustainability, and smart outdoor living, Solorcool may develop a loyal customer base that is less likely to move to competing businesses. When combined, these barriers contribute to creating a strong position in a specific developing market.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
454 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (A. Technological Environment)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
455 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (      Implementation of new technologies such as the 5G network, AI, and IoT could help SolarCool Umbrella enhance its functionalities, creating real-time weather notifications and intelligent cooling controls. With regard to the user experience, future models might integrate with applications. On the flip side, competitors could introduce either cheaper solar technology or faster innovations, thereby disrupting the entire market. Therefore, the company must keep tracking the latest technological trends.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
